Yes, that is correct.

The MongoDB lock data will contain the same logical information as the MySQL design:
- Project
- Entity/row ID
- Lock scope (entity or cell)
- Field key for a cell lock
- User name
- Studio name
- Lock token (stored securely as a hash)
- Created time and expiry time
The difference is only the storage format. MySQL uses rows in tables, while MongoDB uses documents in a lock collection.

For a multi-row edit, multiple lock documents share the same lock token. This allows all selected rows to be managed and released together.

